.css-jfmysa{display:inline;word-break:break-word;} .css-1ankfgb{cursor:pointer;display:inline;word-break:break-word;} Nepal is seeking financial aid following the catastrophic flood that killed hundreds of people and caused billions of dollars in economic losses, tapping a global financial mechanism established to address the impacts of climate change in developing countries.

Kathmandu has formally requested the UN-backed Fund for responding to Loss and Damage to “respond rapidly, directly and effectively” amid the country’s worst natural disaster in decades.

In a letter signed by Finance Minister Swarnim Wagle and Environment Minister Gita Chaudhary, officials said early action by the entity would signal a capability to respond “with humanity, flexibility and solidarity when climate-vulnerable countries face loss and damage beyond their capacity to address alone”.

